--- (b2) the Cargo Book's own 'Verifying Latest Dependencies' recipe, as shipped with rustc 1.94.0 ---
117-Some potential solutions include:
118-Not checking in the Cargo.lock
119-Depending on PR velocity, many versions may go untested
120-This comes at the cost of determinism
121-Have a CI job verify the latest dependencies but mark it to “continue on failure”
122-Depending on the CI service, failures might not be obvious
123-Depending on PR velocity, may use more resources than necessary
124-Have a scheduled CI job to verify latest dependencies
125-A hosted CI service may disable scheduled jobs for repositories that
126-haven’t been touched in a while, affecting passively maintained packages
127-Depending on the CI service, notifications might not be routed to people
128-who can act on the failure
129-If not balanced with dependency publish rate, may not test enough versions
130-or may do redundant testing
131-Regularly update dependencies through PRs, like with Dependabot or RenovateBot
132-Can isolate dependencies to their own PR or roll them up into a single PR
133-Only uses the resources necessary
134-Can configure the frequency to balance CI resources and coverage of dependency versions
135-An example CI job to verify latest dependencies, using GitHub Actions:
136-jobs:
137-latest_deps:
138-name: Latest Dependencies
139-runs-on: ubuntu-latest
140-continue-on-error: true
141-env:
142:CARGO_RESOLVER_INCOMPATIBLE_RUST_VERSIONS: allow
143-steps:
144-- uses: actions/checkout@v4
145-- run: rustup update stable && rustup default stable
146-- run: cargo update --verbose
147-- run: cargo build --verbose
148-- run: cargo test --verbose
149-Notes:
150:CARGO_RESOLVER_INCOMPATIBLE_RUST_VERSIONS is set to ensure the resolver doesn’t limit selected dependencies because of your project’s Rust version .
151-For projects with higher risks of per-platform or per-Rust version failures,
152-more combinations may want to be tested.
153-Verifying rust-version
154-When publishing packages that specify rust-version ,
155-it is important to verify the correctness of that field.
156-Some third-party tools that can help with this include:
157-cargo-msrv
158-cargo-hack
159-An example of one way to do this, using GitHub Actions:
160-jobs:
161-msrv:
162-runs-on: ubuntu-latest
163-steps:
164-- uses: actions/checkout@v4
165-- uses: taiki-e/install-action@cargo-hack
166-- run: cargo hack check --rust-version --workspace --all-targets --ignore-private
167-This tries to balance thoroughness with turnaround time:
168-A single platform is used as most projects are platform-agnostic, trusting platform-specific dependencies to verify their behavior.

